From 3d5508e2c4e2129961ae0cdf2020f3eae707f8f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nex <63727521+LuCiLuna@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:28:10 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 156/167] Input: s6sy761: leave the reset line released, not asserted

reset-gpios is GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW, so the pulse ended with a logical 1, which
asserts the line and holds the controller in reset. The probe then reads the
event stack over that same held-in-reset part and gets -EIO from the GENI
engine, every time, at every address, so it looked like a broken bus rather
than a chip that was never released.

Pulse it the other way round: assert, wait, then deassert and leave it there.
On the OnePlus 9's S6SY792 this is the difference between "geni_i2c
990000.i2c: GPI transfer failed: -5" and a working touchscreen (device id
0x3792, 10 slots, IRQ on GPIO 23 firing).
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c | 10 +++++-----
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
index 7db6e8ef2..b77587f5e 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/s6sy761.c
@@ -304,15 +304,15 @@ static int s6sy761_power_on(struct s6sy761_data *sdata)
 		return ret;
 
 	/*
-	 * Some boards have the reset line wired to the controller even though
-	 * the bootloader may leave it released; pulse it so the controller
-	 * starts from a known state.
+	 * reset-gpios is GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW, so a logical 1 asserts it. Pulse it
+	 * and leave it released: the controller answers nothing while it is
+	 * held in reset.
 	 */
 	if (sdata->reset_gpio) {
-		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sdata->reset_gpio, 0);
-		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
 		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sdata->reset_gpio, 1);
 		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(sdata->reset_gpio, 0);
+		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
 	}
 
 	msleep(140);
